# packages/quantum — the QUANTUM domain as a real prism package (#35/#36 ⋈ #67).
#
# The first PURE-`.ys` domain broken out of the monolith into `packages/<domain>/`
# (`docs/packages-decomposition.md` §7). Unlike `synth` (which depends on a native
# audio crate), quantum needs NO native crate: the quantum substrate — algebraic
# effects (`meta::handle`), Born-rule measurement (`meta::sample`), tensor /
# factorize (`meta::tensor` / `meta::factorize`), and the `Qubits` type with its
# `tensor`/`factorize`/`cnot`/… methods — already rides the std floor (`std_core`).
#
# So quantum's only dependency is std, the IMPLICIT base every package shares
# (`resolver::resolve`'s `base: std_core()`), exactly as `synth` declares only its
# native `audio` edge and lets std ride underneath. A demo runs IN-PROCESS:
#
#     chrysalis run packages/quantum/ys/quantum-teleportation.ys --time 4
#
# No codegen runner (that is the native path); pure `.ys` resolves against std.
#
# The AST-builder helpers (`ys/ast.ys`, imported `from .ast` by the bell /
# interference / effects demos) are co-located as a package-local library until the
# shared `lang-demos` / `examples` package lands (`docs/packages-decomposition.md`
# §4), at which point they become a path dependency. The monolith keeps its own
# `ast.ys` copy for the `hand-built-*` cell demos that have not yet broken out.
def package = {
  name: 'quantum',
  version: '0.1.0',
}
